## Summary

Makes the reconciled Hermes fork practical for smaller local models and heterogeneous inference nodes without restoring obsolete fork core code.

This PR now combines two tightly related deployment layers:

1. reduce the static prompt and schema cost paid by every model request;
2. expose one stable local OpenAI-compatible endpoint that routes the resulting request to the best explicitly configured fleet node.

## Root causes

The upstream reconciliation introduced a much larger root `AGENTS.md`, while Hermes project-context discovery had no concise `.hermes.md` override in this repository. The request prefix also carried a verbose skill catalog and complete tool/parameter descriptions on every turn, which disproportionately consumed 8K–32K local-model contexts.

The reconciliation also displaced the historical `agent/fleet.py`. That implementation was useful as design evidence but could not return unchanged: it contained personal tailnet and host defaults, sequential probing, broad credential-forwarding surfaces, simplistic model/latency routing, and integration assumptions that no longer match current transports, middleware, cache manifests, or configuration APIs.

## Lean project context

Adds a concise root `.hermes.md` that takes precedence over `AGENTS.md` for Hermes sessions. It preserves load-bearing engineering, caching, safety, validation, and fork-recovery rules while directing agents to read the full contributor guide only when a task needs it.

The upstream `AGENTS.md` remains unchanged for humans and other coding assistants.

A repository-boundary regression test requires `.hermes.md` to remain below 8,000 characters and verifies that Hermes selects it instead of `AGENTS.md` even for an 8K context model.

## Small-model context optimizer

Adds the opt-in bundled `small-model-context` plugin using the public `llm_request` middleware contract.

Profiles:

- `lean` — automatically selected at <=32K context; names-only skill catalog and bounded tool/parameter descriptions;
- `balanced` — selected above 32K through 128K; compact skill and schema descriptions;
- `full` — selected above 128K; upstream request remains unchanged;
- explicit `off`, `lean`, `balanced`, or `full` configuration always wins.

When context length is unknown, common model IDs up to 14B select lean, models above 70B select full, and other models select balanced.

The transformation preserves every skill name, tool name, parameter, type, enum, required field, bound, branch, message, and conversation-history item. It transforms a deep copy immediately before dispatch and caches one deterministic policy per session so request-prefix bytes remain stable.

Bundled middleware is discovered alphabetically. The registration entry point therefore lives under `plugins/00-small-model-context/`, while the implementation and operator guide remain under `plugins/small-model-context/`. This guarantees compaction occurs before `cache-foundation` fingerprints the request.

Administration:

```bash
hermes plugins enable small-model-context
hermes context-opt status
hermes context-opt status --json
```

## Local fleet and Headroom router

Adds the opt-in `fleet-router` plugin as a standalone OpenAI-compatible proxy rather than patching provider clients or the agent loop.

Hermes uses one stable endpoint such as:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:8765/v1
```

The proxy owns the explicitly configured node registry and supports:

- concurrent bounded health and model-catalog probes;
- stable client-facing model aliases mapped to node-specific model IDs;
- hard eligibility checks for model availability, tools, vision, reasoning, and context fit;
- tokenizer-independent request sizing that includes tool schemas and all context-bearing fields while replacing base64 image bytes with stable markers;
- scoring by remaining context headroom, exact model match, operator priority, configured prefill/decode throughput, measured latency EMA, current in-flight concurrency, session affinity, and cache-checkpoint affinity;
- bounded retry on connection failures and upstream 5xx responses;
- immediate relay of concrete upstream 4xx responses to avoid duplicated invalid work;
- normal JSON and streaming `/v1/chat/completions` passthrough;
- `/v1/models`, `/health`, and `/fleet/status` endpoints;
- `X-Hermes-Fleet-Node` response attribution.

Administration:

```bash
hermes plugins enable fleet-router
hermes fleet doctor
hermes fleet discover
hermes fleet status --json
hermes fleet route --model local-coder --input-tokens 12000 --max-output-tokens 4000 --tools --reasoning
hermes fleet serve
```

## Fleet safety defaults

- No hostnames, tailnets, ports, subnet scans, or personal machine names are built in.
- Only nodes listed under `fleet.nodes` are contacted.
- Public endpoints are rejected unless that individual node sets `allow_remote: true`.
- The proxy binds to loopback by default.
- A non-loopback bind requires `fleet.listen.allow_non_loopback: true` and can require a dedicated inbound token.
- Inbound `Authorization` is never forwarded upstream.
- A protected node receives a bearer token only from its explicitly named `api_key_env`.
- `OPENAI_API_KEY` and unrelated provider credentials are never forwarded implicitly.
- Hermes cache identity headers may pass through so compatible local controllers can consume exact prefix identities.

